How references are presented, by where they point.

- A reference leaving the value is inlined if concrete (outsideConcrete: 5).
  Otherwise evaluated/documented/final bind it in a let generated at the end,
  named after the referenced path in upper case (INPUT), with the doc comment
  "//cue:path: <path>". That comment is the only way to tell a generated let
  from an author's.
- A reference within the value stays as written in evaluated/documented: to a
  sibling (x: _h), to an author's let (r: L), through self (q: S.p). final
  resolves the sibling and self, but keeps the author's let.
- raw leaves references dangling as written (_R, ext, input.y), except that a
  let declared outside the value is inlined (outsideLet).
- written shows only the reference where contents come from elsewhere
  (viaReference: <nil>, _R), operands of & separately (viaUnify), each
  conjunct of a field declared twice (splitConjuncts), and for a single
  conjunct the whole field, label and comments included.
- Unused lets and aliases are compile errors in language v0.18, so they never
  appear.
-- in.cue --
input: _
ext:   5
_R: {a: input.a, if input.x != _|_ {extra: 1}}
let OUT = {if input.x != _|_ {extra: 1}}

cases: {
	literal:         {a: input.a, if input.x != _|_ {extra: 1}}
	viaReference:    _R
	viaEmbed:        {_R}
	viaUnify:        _R & {}
	splitConjuncts:  {a: input.a}
	splitConjuncts:  {b: 1}
	sibling:         {_h: input.a, x: _h}
	innerLet:        {let L = input.a, r: L}
	outsideLet:      {r: OUT}
	self:            {let S = self, p: 1, q: S.p}
	outsideConcrete: {r: ext}
	outsideIncomplete: {r: input.y}
}
